.Dd September 19, 2026
.Dt STAT 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm stat ,
.Nm lstat ,
.Nm fstat ,
.Nm fstatat ,
.Nm stat64 ,
.Nm lstat64 ,
.Nm fstat64 ,
.Nm fstatat64
.Nd get file attributes
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/stat.h
.Ft int
.Fn stat "const char *restrict path" "struct stat *restrict buf"
.Ft int
.Fn lstat "const char *restrict path" "struct stat *restrict buf"
.Ft int
.Fn fstat "int fd" "struct stat *buf"
.Ft int
.Fn fstatat "int dirfd" "const char *restrict path" "struct stat *restrict buf" "int flags"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn stat
reports the attributes of
.Fa path
in
.Fa buf ,
following a final symbolic link.
.Fn lstat
reports the link itself.
Both follow links in preceding components.
.Fn fstat
reports the object referred to by
.Fa fd .
An open KRFFS root directory descriptor reports the root directory.
.Pp
.Fn fstatat
resolves a relative
.Fa path
from the directory referred to by
.Fa dirfd ,
or from the current working directory when
.Fa dirfd
is
.Dv AT_FDCWD
(-100).
An absolute path ignores
.Fa dirfd .
The descriptor is converted to a path, so ancestor search permissions
still apply.
The copied path, including any directory prefix and terminating null
byte, must fit 256 bytes.
.Pp
.Dv AT_SYMLINK_NOFOLLOW
in
.Fa flags
selects the final symbolic link itself, on ext2 or under
.Pa /proc .
Without it the link is followed.
The kernel also accepts
.Dv AT_NO_AUTOMOUNT
and
.Dv AT_EMPTY_PATH
as flag bits, but they have no effect.
An empty path returns
.Er ENOENT
at the kernel interface.
Musl handles an empty path with exactly
.Dv AT_EMPTY_PATH
and a nonnegative descriptor through
.Fn fstat .
Other flag bits return
.Er EINVAL ,
except when the copied path is
.Pa /
on a KRFFS root, where the kernel does not check flags.
.Ss Reported attributes
.Va st_mode
contains the file type and permission bits;
.Va st_uid
and
.Va st_gid
are its owner and group.
.Va st_nlink
is the reported link count,
.Va st_size
the byte size, and
.Va st_ino
the inode identity.
.Va st_dev
identifies the file system;
.Va st_rdev
identifies a device node's device.
.Pp
The kernel reports an I/O block size of 4096 in
.Va st_blksize .
For stored files,
.Va st_blocks
is the byte size rounded up to 512-byte units, including on ext2.
It does not measure the blocks actually allocated to a sparse file.
.Bl -tag -width "Mounted KRFFS"
.It KRFFS root
Nodes other than the root directory report stored owner, group, size,
and access, modification, and attribute-change times, including nanoseconds.
The link count is 1.
The root directory has inode 1, mode 0755, and its own stored times;
without a mounted KRFFS image, the time helper uses boot time.
The file-system device number is 0.
.Pp
The root reports inode 1 through path and descriptor calls, the old
.Nm stat
call (106), and the VFS attribute call, including when a path reaches it
through
.Pa .. ,
as in
.Pa /tmp/.. .
Other directories report their stored ids.
A file's inode number combines its directory's id with the file's stored id,
the 32-bit DJB hash of its name.
Directory listings contain neither
.Pa .
nor
.Pa .. ;
.Nm getdents
reports each entry's inode number by these same rules.
.Pp
.Fn unlink
clears an open file's name but retains its stored id and directory,
so its inode number stays unchanged while it is open.
Two open files with distinct numbers retain them after both are unlinked.
.Fn rename
rewrites a file's stored id, so renaming it changes its inode number;
Linux preserves the number across a rename.
Two names in one directory whose 32-bit hashes collide share an inode
number, unlike Linux's distinct inode numbers for distinct files in one
file system.
.It Mounted KRFFS
Attributes and times come from the stored node.
Directories report two links and files one.
The inode is the node's byte offset plus one; relocation changes it.
.It ext2
Owner, group, mode, link count, size, and the three times come from the inode.
Times have second precision.
.Fn lstat
reports a symbolic link's type and target length.
Device inodes report their stored device numbers even though they cannot
be opened as devices.
.It FAT32
Owner and group are the mount's values.
Permissions are 0777 with the mount mask removed; the read-only attribute
also removes write bits.
The link count is 1 and the size is the directory entry's stored size.
Modification time has two-second precision, access time has date precision,
and creation time is reported as
.Va st_ctim
with hundredths of a second.
Stored times are interpreted as UTC.
.It exFAT
Owner, group, permissions, and link count follow the FAT32 rules.
The size is the stored data length.
Creation time is reported as
.Va st_ctim .
Creation and modification times include the stored 10 ms increments.
A valid stored UTC offset is applied; otherwise the time is treated as UTC.
.El
.Pp
Reads do not advance access times.
An ext2, FAT32, exFAT, or additional KRFFS mount reports
.Va st_dev
as 0x10 plus their mount-table index, including an ext2 root at index 0.
.Ss Synthetic objects
.Pa /dev
uses
.Va st_dev
5 and
.Pa /proc
uses 6.
Synthetic objects, sockets, and pipes report boot time for all three
times, zero nanoseconds, one link, and zero size.
Proc text files report zero size even when reading them returns data.
Unfollowed proc links report symbolic-link type and mode 0777.
.Pp
A socket or pipe descriptor reports the object's own inode.
A followed
.Pa /proc/<pid>/fd/<n>
link reports the target descriptor's attributes, so its inode agrees
with
.Fn fstat .
Proc-file descriptors use their source files' inode numbers; a master
descriptor opened through
.Pa /dev/ptmx
reports zero for
.Fa st_dev ,
.Fa st_rdev
and
.Fa st_ino .
.Pp
Device numbers use the major number shifted left eight bits, combined
with the minor number.
The named device nodes have these major and minor numbers:
.Bl -column "loop0 through loop3" "Major" "Minor" -offset indent
.It Sy Node Ta Sy Major Ta Sy Minor
.It Pa null Ta 1 Ta 3
.It Pa zero Ta 1 Ta 5
.It Pa full Ta 1 Ta 7
.It Pa random Ta 1 Ta 8
.It Pa urandom Ta 1 Ta 9
.It Pa tty Ta 5 Ta 0
.It Pa console Ta 5 Ta 1
.It Pa ptmx Ta 5 Ta 2
.It Pa pts/N Ta 136 Ta N
.It Pa bootpart Ta 240 Ta 0
.It Pa disk0 Ta 240 Ta 1
.It Pa bootlog Ta 240 Ta 2
.It Pa grubcore Ta 240 Ta 3
.It Pa loop0 No through Pa loop3 Ta 240 Ta 32 through 35
.It Pa loop-control Ta 240 Ta 36
.It Pa framefail Ta 240 Ta 37
.It Pa screenlog Ta 240 Ta 38
.El
.Pp
Names in the table are relative to
.Pa /dev .
For
.Pa disk0pM
with M from 1 through 4, the minor is 3 + M.
Other
.Pa diskN
and
.Pa diskNpM
nodes use minor 64 + 16*N + M, with M zero for a whole disk.
Disk nodes are block devices; the other listed device nodes are
character devices.
Only devices present in the namespace can be queried.
.Ss System call interface
The i386 calls are
.Nm stat
(106),
.Nm lstat
(107),
.Nm stat64
(195),
.Nm lstat64
(196),
.Nm fstat64
(197), and
.Nm fstatat64
(300).
Path calls take the path in EBX and result pointer in ECX;
.Nm fstat64
takes the descriptor in EBX and result pointer in ECX.
.Nm fstatat64
takes directory descriptor, path, result pointer, and flags in EBX,
ECX, EDX, and ESI.
.Pp
Musl tries
.Nm statx
and, on
.Er ENOSYS ,
uses the 64-bit stat calls.
It maps
.Fn stat ,
.Fn lstat ,
.Fn fstat ,
and
.Fn fstatat
to the corresponding 64-bit kernel interfaces.
With
.Dv _LARGEFILE64_SOURCE ,
musl defines
.Nm stat64 , lstat64 , fstat64 ,
and
.Nm fstatat64
as macros for these public functions; they have no separate prototypes.
.Pp
The kernel stat64 result is 96 bytes in the i386 layout.
Its fields, in order, are:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
uint64_t st_dev;
uint8_t  pad0[4];
uint32_t old_st_ino;
uint32_t st_mode, st_nlink, st_uid, st_gid;
uint64_t st_rdev;
uint8_t  pad3[4];
int64_t  st_size;
uint32_t st_blksize;
uint64_t st_blocks;
uint32_t st_atime, st_atime_nsec;
uint32_t st_mtime, st_mtime_nsec;
uint32_t st_ctime, st_ctime_nsec;
uint64_t st_ino;
.Ed
.Pp
The final inode field is at byte offset 88.
Musl converts this structure to its public
.Vt struct stat ,
which has 64-bit time fields.
The attributes above are the 64-bit interfaces'.
Calls 106 and 107 instead fill the older, narrower structure with
16-bit mode, link count, and owner fields and 32-bit sizes and inode numbers.
They do not check for truncation when converting wider attributes, they
report the fixed attributes of the boot root for
.Pa /
even on an ext2 root, and they leave
.Fa st_rdev
zero for a device inode on a mounted volume.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
These functions return 0 on success.
On failure they return -1 and set
.Va errno .
The kernel calls return negative error numbers on failure.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/vfsprobe.c
checks that
.Nm stat64
reports a mode set by
.Xr chmod 2
and a modification time set by
.Xr utimensat 2 .
.Pa tests/procprobe.c
checks that a socket's
.Nm fstat64
inode agrees with its proc descriptor link and TCP listing.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width "[ENAMETOOLONG]"
.It Bq Er EACCES
Search permission on an ancestor directory was denied.
.It Bq Er EBADF
The descriptor is invalid, or a relative path uses a removed directory
descriptor.
.It Bq Er EBUSY
Path resolution entered a mount being mounted or unmounted.
.It Bq Er EFAULT
A path or result pointer cannot be accessed as required, is null,
or there is no current process.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
Unsupported
.Fn fstatat
flag bits were supplied, or required attributes cannot be obtained.
.It Bq Er EIO
File-system metadata or I/O failed, or a mount's device was removed.
.It Bq Er ELOOP
Path resolution would follow more than 40 symbolic links.
.It Bq Er ENAMETOOLONG
A copied or reconstructed path, component, or symbolic-link expansion
exceeds its buffer.
.It Bq Er ENOENT
The path is empty, a required component is absent, or a synthetic entry
or process no longer exists.
.It Bq Er ENOMEM
A node needed for path resolution cannot be allocated.
.It Bq Er ENOTDIR
A path component or relative-path base descriptor is not a directory.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr chmod 2 ,
.Xr chown 2 ,
.Xr open 2 ,
.Xr statfs 2 ,
.Xr symlink 2 ,
.Xr utimensat 2 ,
.Xr proc 5
